 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(a^3)$ per sample, where $a$ is the kernel order. The split-axis GPU implementation requires a fixed number of passes per axis (e.g., 43 passes for $N=3$) rather than a naive $O(N^3)$ approach [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ for standard CPU/GPU texture sampling. The split-axis GPU method requires storing intermediate textures, increasing memory usage proportional to the number of passes and texture dimensions [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Experiments show the architecture using this resampling can reach high PSNR [S1, unofficial]. Not given in the sources regarding comparative performance against lower-order methods.
-*   **Strengths:** Sharp reconstruction with minimal blurring; effective for high-resolution medical image compression and reconstruction [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(a^3)$ per sample, where $a$ is the kernel order [G]. The split-axis GPU implementation requires a fixed number of passes per axis (e.g., 43 passes for $N=3$) rather than a naive $O(N^3)$ approach [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ for standard CPU/GPU texture sampling [G]. The split-axis GPU method requires storing intermediate textures, increasing memory usage proportional to the number of passes and texture dimensions [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Experiments show the architecture using this resampling can reach high PSNR [S1, unofficial]. Comparative performance against lower-order methods is not given in the sources [G].
+*   **Strengths:** High PSNR reconstruction for medical images [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Limitations:** The split-axis GPU variant requires multiple rendering passes and intermediate texture storage, increasing computational and memory overhead [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Lanczos resampling is named after the mathematician Cornelius Lanczos. In the context of volume rendering, it is applied to compress high-resolution medical images using Implicit Neural Representations (INRs) like SIREN, where it serves as a preprocessing step to downsample HR images before neural network training [S1, unofficial]. It has also been used in Fourier volume rendering on the GPU to implement windowed-sinc functions for extracting planes in frequency space [S2, unofficial].
